+
+# Keep post revisions on every edit
+
+## Context
+
+Saving the edit form overwrote a post for good. Authors lost published
+content by pasting the wrong draft over it, with no way back.
+
+## Decision
+
+Every successful edit or restore keeps the title, excerpt and body the post
+had *before* the write as a row in `post_revisions`. The snapshot and the
+update run in one transaction (`Post.keepRevisionAndUpdate`), so a post can
+never change without its previous version being kept. Creating a post keeps
+nothing; a refused edit keeps nothing.
+
+Restoring is itself an edit: it snapshots the current version first, so the
+restored revision stays in the list and nothing is ever lost.
+
+Revisions are private to the author. `PostPolicy` gates `viewRevisions` and
+`restore` exactly like `update`. A restore looks the revision up scoped to
+the post in the URL, so a revision id from another post is a 404 even when
+the same author owns both.
+
+Deleting a post deletes its revisions explicitly in the same transaction.
+The FK also declares `ON DELETE CASCADE`, but SQLite only honours it when
+`PRAGMA foreign_keys` is on, which the app connection does not guarantee.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Authors can undo any edit from `/posts/:id/revisions`. Storage grows by one
+row per edit; pruning and side-by-side comparison are out of scope for now.
